# Overview

This project is a WordPress website with an embedded React application at the '/directory' endpoint. The React app is a user directory and jobs board, which allows users to fill out their profiles and relevant work experience so that they might appear in another user's candidate searches.

# Tech Stack

We use Docker Compose to host this site. The stack consists of WordPress (php-fpm), Apache, WP-CLI, MariaDB, and Redis containers.

Core site components:
  - WordPress
  - PHP 8.2+
  - WP GraphQL (https://www.wpgraphql.com/)
  - React 19

We're using the https://wordpress.org/plugins/reactpress/ plugin to facilitate hosting the React app inside the WordPress frontend structure.

# Project Structure

The WordPress backend is in the `wp-content/` directory. The majority of our site's functionality is in the `rise` mu-plugin.

The React frontend is in the `wp-content/reactpress/apps/rise-frontend` directory.

## Workspace Paths

This repo maintains parallel copies of the same stack. Confirm which path is active before editing:

- `gaswirth@rhdev/wp-content/` — primary dev environment
- `site_files/wp-content/` — alternate/synced copy used by some deployments

The `rise` mu-plugin and `rise-frontend` React app may exist in both trees. Apply fixes to the active environment first; mirror to the other copy if they have diverged.

Respect stated edit-scope constraints (e.g. frontend-only) until the user explicitly expands permission to backend paths.

# WP GraphQL Conventions

Custom GraphQL fields typed as taxonomy term types (`Union`, `Location`, `Skill`, etc.) must resolve to `WPGraphQL\Model\Term` instances — not plain PHP arrays with `databaseId`/`name`/`slug` keys. WPGraphQL's `TermNode` interface rejects arrays and throws:

`Argument #1 ($term) must be of type WPGraphQL\Model\Term, array given`

When a frontend query errors on `TermNode`, trace custom resolvers in `rise/src/Includes/GraphQLQueries.php` (especially `prepare_taxonomy_terms()` and `get_job_skills()`) before attempting frontend workarounds. Frontend query changes cannot fix a resolver returning the wrong PHP type.

